Q: `reinterpret_cast` between unrelated structs A: <p>The Standard specifies that a <code>reinterpret_cast</code> between object pointers is the equivalent of a double <code>static_cast</code> from <code>A*</code> to <code>void*</code> and then to <code>B*</code>. ([expr.reinterpret.cast]/7) When the types are unrelated but have the same object representations, It will work on compilers in 2026, but there could be a standard-conforming implementation where it does not.  For example, the runtime is allowed to keep track of which objects it created where, and crash if the program attempts to access a memory address through an incompatible pointer. It’s even plausible to me that an implementation designed for memory safety would.</p>
<p>One standard way to do this is to make each <code>struct</code> (which must be a C-like “standard-layout <code>struct</code>,” not something complex like an object with <code>virtual</code> functions or inheritance) a member of a <code>union</code>.  If the first one or more members of the structures are layout-compatible, you can then type-pun on those members through a different <code>struct</code> in the <code>union</code>.  To quote the Standard ([class.union.general], note 1):</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One special guarantee is made in order to simplify the use of unions: If a standard-layout union contains several standard-layout structs that share a common initial sequence, and if a non-static data member of an object of this standard-layout union type is active and is one of the standard-layout structs, it is permitted to inspect the common initial sequence of any of the standard-layout struct members</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So for example,</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>    union {
        A a = {.x = 1, .y = 2.0f, .z = 3.0, .arr = {}};
        B b;
    }; // Type-pun from A to B through an anonymous union.

    std::cout &lt;&lt; b.x &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b.y &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; b.z &lt;&lt; '\n';
</code></pre>
<p>If the structures can be type-punned by copying their object representations with <code>memcpy()</code>, you can also convert from one to the other using <code>std::bit_cast</code>.  If the source object is correctly-aligned for the type you want to convert to, <code>std::start_lifetime_as</code> is how you replace an object with a new object of a different type, at the same memory location, with the same object representation. (This <strong>does not</strong> let you break the Strict-Aliasing Rule: the original object that was there is replaced.) See [obj.lifetime].</p>

Q: Finding row with minimum sum in matrix outputs first row instead of correct one A: <p>Here is another proposal that might introduce <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/ranges.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::ranges</code></a> to you in case you don't know them. Suppose we have the following vector</p>
<pre><code>std::vector&lt;std::vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; arr  = {
    {  1,  3,   2, 54, 234},
    { 75, 12,   3, 46,   9},
    { 13, 26,  56,  9,  12},
    { 14, 90, 897,  6,  34}
};
</code></pre>
<p>Then, one can define a view <code>vw</code> that (1) iterates each row of this vector, then (2) transforms each row by computing the sum of its item and then (3) associates the index of the row for having a tuple <code>(idx,sum)</code>:</p>
<pre><code>auto vw = arr   // (1) iterate each item of arr
    | std::views::transform([](auto const&amp; row)  {  // (2) compute the sum
        return std::accumulate(row.begin(),row.end(), int{0}); })
    | std::views::enumerate  // (3) associate the index of the row to the sum
;
</code></pre>
<p>From there, you can call the <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/algorithm/ranges/min.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::ranges::min</code></a> for computing the minimum. Since each iterated item is a tuple <code>(idx,sum)</code>, one has to sort according to the second item of the tuple, which can be done by providing a projection as third argument to <code>std::ranges::min</code></p>
<pre><code>auto [idx,sum] = std::ranges::min (vw, {}, [](auto t) { return std::get&lt;1&gt;(t); });
</code></pre>
<p>Note how one can use structured binding in order to directly get the expected index as <code>idx</code> and the sum as <code>sum</code>.</p>
<p>Since the above code implies usage of C++23, we also can use <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/io/println.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::println</code></a> for the final display:</p>
<pre><code>std::println (&quot;idx: {}  sum: {}   row: {} &quot;, idx, sum, arr[idx]);
</code></pre>
<p>which should produce the following output:</p>
<pre><code>idx: 2  sum: 116   row: [13, 26, 56, 9, 12]
</code></pre>
<p>The full code will be:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;vector&gt;
#include &lt;ranges&gt;
#include &lt;numeric&gt;
#include &lt;print&gt;

int main()
{
    std::vector&lt;std::vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; arr  = {
        {  1,  3,   2, 54, 234},
        { 75, 12,   3, 46,   9},
        { 13, 26,  56,  9,  12},
        { 14, 90, 897,  6,  34}
    };

    auto vw = arr
        | std::views::transform([](auto const&amp; row)  {
            return std::accumulate(row.begin(),row.end(), int{0}); })
        | std::views::enumerate
    ;

    auto [idx,sum] = std::ranges::min (vw, {}, [](auto t) { return std::get&lt;1&gt;(t); });

    std::println (&quot;idx: {}  sum: {}   row: {} &quot;, idx, sum, arr[idx]);
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/f3rGcWvev" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>

Q: Implement trace-id with Spring Webflux A: <p>Sleuth 3.0 provides automatic instrumentation for WebFlux, which means, if you do nothing, you will always get the current span of the thread which subscribes to your Mono or Flux. If you wish to overwrite this, for instance because you are batching a lot of requests and want a unique trace for every transaction, all you have to do is to manipulate the Context of your operator chain.</p>
<pre><code>private Mono&lt;Data&gt; performRequest() {
    var span = tracer.spanBuilder().setNoParent().start(); // generate a completely new trace
                                                           // note: you can also just generate a new span if you want
    return Mono.defer(() -&gt; callRealService())
        .contextWrite(Context.of(TraceContext.class, span.context());
}
</code></pre>
<p>When following this approach, make sure to import <code>org.springframework.cloud.sleuth.Tracer</code> and not the one by brave, because they use different types and Reactor will drop your Mono with an ugly error message if they don't align correctly (unfortunately, since the Context ist just a plain old <code>Map&lt;Object, Object&gt;</code>, you won't get a compiler error).</p>
